<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bosnian War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1992">1992</a>–<a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bosnian War was fought from 1992 to 1995 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> after its declaration of independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, among Bosniak, Bosnian Serb and Bosnian Croat forces. It saw the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a>, ethnic cleansing and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srebrenica">Srebrenica</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of 1995. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> air strikes and Croatian-Bosniak offensives led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayton-agreement">Dayton Agreement</a> of December 1995, which ended the war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bosnian-war.jpg" alt="Bosnian War" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Bushido at bs.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krupa_nakon_rata.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war arose from the breakup of the Socialist Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. The League of Communists of Yugoslavia had lost its ideological force at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, and ethnic nationalism had revived in the 1980s after violence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> seceded in 1991, the multi-ethnic Socialist Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>, inhabited mainly by Muslim Bosniaks (44%), Orthodox Serbs (32.5%) and Catholic Croats (17%), held a referendum on independence on 29 February 1992. Bosnian Serb representatives boycotted it and rejected the result, having already proclaimed their own Serb republic on 9 January 1992.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The war is commonly dated from 6 April 1992, when the new republic was internationally recognised. Bosnian Serb forces led by Radovan Karadžić, supported by the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic">Slobodan Milošević</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and supplied by the Yugoslav People's Army, seized about 70% of the country in a campaign marked by widespread ethnic cleansing. Serb forces began shelling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> on 6 April. Croat forces formed the Croatian Defence Council on 8 April, and tensions between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosniaks">Bosniaks</a> led to the Croat-Bosniak War in early 1993.</p><p>In 1994 the Washington Agreement created the Federation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> and allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosniaks">Bosniaks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> against the Republika Srpska. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srebrenica">Srebrenica</a> and Markale massacres, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>'s Operation Deliberate Force in 1995 targeted the Serb army and proved key in ending the war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayton-agreement">Dayton Accords</a> were initialled on 21 November 1995.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Estimates of the dead range widely, from 25,000 to 329,000, depending on who is counted. A database published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> in 2007 names at least 97,207 people killed or missing, and over 2.2 million people were displaced. An estimated 12,000 to 50,000 women were raped, mostly Bosniak women and mainly by Serb forces. The killing of over 8,000 Bosniak males at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srebrenica">Srebrenica</a> in July 1995 by Serb forces is the only incident in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> recognised as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. In 2007 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-court-of-justice">International Court of Justice</a> found that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> had failed to prevent it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bosnian War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1995, after the Croatian Army's Operation Storm and NATO's involvement in the <b>Bosnian War</b>, Milošević took over the negotiating powers of all the Serb secessionist movements.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
